<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medinaceli</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Medinaceli is a small hilltop town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalon">Jalón</a> valley. It keeps a Roman triumphal arch and was a key fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, the base of its Middle March against the Christian kingdoms; the chamberlain al-Mansur died there in 1002. It later gave its name to the dukes of Medinaceli, one of Spain's leading noble houses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medinaceli.jpg" alt="Medinaceli" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olga Gairin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinaceli_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Medinaceli stands on a hilltop at about 1210 metres above sea level, at the confluence of the rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalon">Jalón</a> and Arbujuelo, and looks over the Jalón valley. The municipality includes other villages, among them Torralba del Moral, and two motorways, the A-15 and the A-2, meet within its bounds. Its name comes from the Arabic madinat Salim, City of Salim, which goes back to the Banu Salim, a lineage of the Berber Masmuda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Islamic period the site was a Celtiberian town known as Occilis or Okilis. Under Islamic rule it held the status of a madina, a city. The Berber Banu Salim settled in the area, and although they lost favour and were routed in 920, the town kept its strategic importance for the Cordobese authorities. It served as a powerbase for military operations in the north, including the campaign against the Christian settlements of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a> and the suppression of the revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tujibids">Banu Tujib</a>. In 946 Medinaceli became the head of the Middle March, the frontier district in this region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town has a Roman arch built between the 1st and 3rd centuries CE, the only three-gated Roman arch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which is used as a symbol of Spain's historic sites across the country. The castle was the residence of the Dukes of Medinaceli until the Ducal Palace took over that purpose. Other buildings include the Colegiata de Nuestra Señora de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, whose abbots disputed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Sigüenza to defend the city's rights, and the 16th-century Convent of Saint Elizabeth beside the church of San Martín. The beaterio of San Román is in ruins and may once have been a synagogue. A stone gate is one of the few remains of the old city walls dating from the Islamic period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Medinaceli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Christian north was held in check from three fortified frontier marches based on Zaragoza, <b>Medinaceli</b> and Mérida, and fortresses such as Gormaz, rebuilt in the 960s, guarded the Duero frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The title later passed to branches of the royal family and then to the Dukes of Segorbe and <b>Medinaceli</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-empuries">County of Empúries</a></li></ul></section>
